WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.430
Turn your phone sideways, and

2
00:00:02.430 --> 00:00:03.510
the video turns too.

3
00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:05.790
Hold it to your ear in a call,

4
00:00:06.010 --> 00:00:07.490
and the screen goes dark.

5
00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:09.100
Who's telling it?

6
00:00:09.670 --> 00:00:10.530
Its sensors.

7
00:00:12.870 --> 00:00:13.810
Let's find them.

8
00:00:14.950 --> 00:00:18.110
Today: the sensors in your phone, and how

9
00:00:18.110 --> 00:00:20.310
your camera turns light into numbers.

10
00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:23.300
First, the motion sensor,

11
00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:24.780
or accelerometer.

12
00:00:25.410 --> 00:00:27.450
It feels which way is down,

13
00:00:27.710 --> 00:00:29.910
and every shake and step.

14
00:00:30.590 --> 00:00:32.330
That's how your screen turns

15
00:00:32.330 --> 00:00:33.650
and steps get counted.

16
00:00:34.910 --> 00:00:36.210
Near the top of the screen

17
00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:38.070
sit two tiny sensors.

18
00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:41.140
The light sensor measures how bright it

19
00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:43.340
is, and sets the screen to match.

20
00:00:44.090 --> 00:00:47.030
The proximity sensor, the nearness sensor,

21
00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:49.810
notices when something is very close.

22
00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:51.800
There's more.

23
00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:54.600
A compass sensor feels the Earth's

24
00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:57.080
magnetism, so maps point the right way.

25
00:00:57.640 --> 00:00:59.700
The GPS listens to satellites

26
00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:00.960
to find where you are.

27
00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:03.570
Microphones hear you, and the

28
00:01:03.570 --> 00:01:05.330
touch screen feels your finger.

29
00:01:06.570 --> 00:01:07.030
Quick check!

30
00:01:07.690 --> 00:01:09.130
You're on a call, and the

31
00:01:09.130 --> 00:01:10.670
screen goes dark at your ear.

32
00:01:11.290 --> 00:01:13.390
Which sensor noticed your ear?

33
00:01:14.660 --> 00:01:15.760
The proximity sensor.

34
00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:18.380
On most phones, it sends out

35
00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:20.860
invisible infrared light and

36
00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:22.740
notices it bounce back off your ear.

37
00:01:23.330 --> 00:01:25.330
Then the screen switches off, so

38
00:01:25.330 --> 00:01:26.830
your cheek can't tap buttons.

39
00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:29.840
Now the biggest sensor in

40
00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:31.180
your phone: the camera.

41
00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:33.740
It's millions of tiny light

42
00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:36.040
sensors in a grid, each measuring

43
00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:37.500
the light that lands on it.

44
00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:40.420
A twelve-megapixel camera has

45
00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:41.460
about twelve million.

46
00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:45.800
Each tiny square of a photo is a pixel.

47
00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:49.410
Your phone stores each pixel as numbers

48
00:01:49.410 --> 00:01:51.850
for its red, green and blue light.

49
00:01:52.490 --> 00:01:54.990
So to your phone, a photo

50
00:01:54.990 --> 00:01:56.790
is a grid of numbers.

51
00:01:57.620 --> 00:01:59.720
Change them, and the picture changes.

52
00:02:01.130 --> 00:02:03.610
It's like a mosaic of small tiles.

53
00:02:04.280 --> 00:02:06.620
Close up, you see only squares.

54
00:02:07.630 --> 00:02:09.670
Step back, and you see a face.

55
00:02:10.460 --> 00:02:12.620
Zoom into a photo, and you'll

56
00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:13.820
find the squares too.

57
00:02:15.090 --> 00:02:16.590
You can read your phone's

58
00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:17.270
sensors yourself.

59
00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:20.600
A free app called phyphox shows

60
00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:21.900
them as numbers and graphs.

61
00:02:22.630 --> 00:02:26.250
Measure how loud break is, how bright the

62
00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:29.230
window is, or whether your desk is level.

63
00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:30.950
Your turn.

64
00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:32.940
You take a photo with a

65
00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:34.500
twelve-megapixel camera.

66
00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:37.460
What does your phone actually store?

67
00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:40.560
About twelve million pixels,

68
00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:42.280
each stored as numbers.

69
00:02:43.070 --> 00:02:45.870
Remember: motion, proximity and

70
00:02:45.870 --> 00:02:48.410
light sensors, a compass and GPS.

71
00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:51.820
And a camera: millions of light

72
00:02:51.820 --> 00:02:54.020
sensors making a grid of numbers.

73
00:02:55.420 --> 00:02:58.160
Try it tomorrow: with a parent's OK,

74
00:02:58.780 --> 00:03:01.760
open phyphox or a compass app, and

75
00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:03.680
measure one thing in two places.

76
00:03:04.460 --> 00:03:06.480
Write both numbers with their units.

77
00:03:07.290 --> 00:03:07.890
No phone?

78
00:03:08.670 --> 00:03:10.830
Draw a picture on squared paper,

79
00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:12.890
with a number in each square.

80
00:03:13.570 --> 00:03:15.410
Next time: measuring it right.
